## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a memory-bounded streaming algorithm for heavy hitters with a fixed-coverage threshold.

## Key Concepts
- **Heavy Hitters:** Values that appear frequently in the stream
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Process data incrementally without storing the entire dataset
- **Bounded Memory:** Fixed-size data structures regardless of stream length
- **Threshold-Based Reporting:** Selecting the minimum subset that meets a coverage target

## Algorithm Analysis
- **Per-Element Update Cost:** O(log k) for heap maintenance (hash map lookup is O(1) average)
- **Per-Batch Query Cost:** O(k log k) to sort the heap and accumulate to the 95% threshold
- **Memory Usage:** O(M) for counts (if M is small) or O(unique values) with a hash map, plus O(k) for the heap

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.heap = [];
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }
  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this.bubbleUp(this.heap.length - 1);
    if (this.heap.length > this.capacity) this.pop();
  }
  pop() {
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const end = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = end;
      this.bubbleDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }
  bubbleUp(i) {
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);
      if (this.heap[p].count <= this.heap[i].count) break;
      [this.heap[p], this.heap[i]] = [this.heap[i], this.heap[p]];
      i = p;
    }
  }
  bubbleDown(i) {
    while (true) {
      const l = 2 * i + 1, r = 2 * i + 2;
      let smallest = i;
      if (l < this.heap.length && this.heap[l].count < this.heap[smallest].count) smallest = l;
      if (r < this.heap.length && this.heap[r].count < this.heap[smallest].count) smallest = r;
      if (smallest === i) break;
      [this.heap[i], this.heap[smallest]] = [this.heap[smallest], this.heap[i]];
      i = smallest;
    }
  }
}

const counts = new Map();
let total = 0;
const k = 20;
const heap = new MinHeap(k);

function updateBatch(batch) {
  for (const v of batch) {
    const count = (counts.get(v) || 0) + 1;
    counts.set(v, count);
    total++;
    heap.push({ value: v, count });
  }
}

function get95Cover() {
  const sorted = [...heap.heap].sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);
  const threshold = total * 0.95;
  let sum = 0;
  const result = [];
  for (const item of sorted) {
    sum += item.count;
    result.push(item.value);
    if (sum >= threshold) break;
  }
  if (sum < threshold) {
    return [...counts.keys()];
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Trade-offs
The heap-only approach tracks only the top-k values, so if fewer than k values dominate the stream, we may not reach 95% coverage. In such cases, the algorithm falls back to returning all seen values. For guaranteed bounded memory with approximate coverage, consider probabilistic methods like Count-Min Sketch or Lossy Counting.

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming frequency sketches are common in prod telemetry. This question probes knowledge of CMS, accuracy guarantees, batch processing, and amortized space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Count-Min Sketch (CMS)
- ε, δ error bounds
- Update/query complexity for batches
- Space complexity O(wd)
- Trade-offs vs exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
// CMS skeleton
const w = Math.ceil(Math.E / eps);
const d = Math.ceil(Math.log(1/delta));
const table = Array.from({length: d}, () => Array(w).fill(0));

function update(x) { for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]++; }

function query(x) { let m = Infinity; for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) m = Math.min(m, table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]); return m; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would deletions or non-monotone streams affect CMS choices?
- How would you monitor actual error in production without degrading performance?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory and concrete trade-offs between accuracy, memory, and speed.

## Key Concepts
- Cardinality estimation in streams
- HyperLogLog algorithm and error scaling
- Trade-offs: memory vs. accuracy vs. per-item cost

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m) {
    this.m = m; // must be power of two
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const x = BigInt("0x" + sha1(item));
    const idx = Number((x >> 64n) & BigInt(this.m - 1));
    const w = Number((x & ((1n << 64n) - 1n)) | 0n);
    const rank = this._rho(w);
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], rank);
  }
  _rho(v) {
    let r = 1;
    while (((v >> (64 - r)) & 1) === 0 && r <= 64) r++;
    return r;
  }
  estimate() {
    // simplified estimate; actual implementation uses harmonic mean with bias corrections
    const Z = Array.from(this.registers).reduce((a,b)=>a+Math.pow(2,-b),0);
    const alpha = this.m ? 0.7213/(1+1.079/this.m) : 0;
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / Z;
  }
}
```

